#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "2393d607-0f73-59f3-9a5f-ebe45050dda9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10106" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.4,\\, -0.3,\\, -2.2,\\, -0.4$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 1\n$$\n\nfor which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}e^{- (x_{i} - θ)^{2}/2}) \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- ∑(x_{i} - θ)^{2}/2} \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- (∑x_{i}^{2} - 2θ∑x_{i} + nθ^{2})/2} \\\\\n&= (2π)^{-n/2}e^{- (C - 2Sθ + nθ^{2})/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 0.4 + (-0.3) + (-2.2) + (-0.4) \\\\\n&=-2.5 \\\\\nC &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= (0.4)^{2} + (-0.3)^{2} + (-2.2)^{2} + (-0.4)^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= (2π)^{-4/2}e^{- (5.25 - 2(-2.5)θ + 4θ^{2})/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4π^{2}}e^{-2.625 - 2.5θ - 2θ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff20920-30c5-5027-b461-31b13d2031e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11121" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5, \\, 2.0, \\, 1.1, \\, 0.4, \\, 3.3, \\, 2.7$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)θe^{- θx_{i}} \\\\\n&= (θe^{- θx_{1}}) \\cdot (θe^{- θx_{2}})⋯(θe^{- θx_{n}}) \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{- θ(x_{1} + x_{2} + ⋯ + x_{n})} \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{- θ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= θ^{n}e^{-Sθ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 1.5 + 2.0 + 1.1 + 0.4 + 3.3 + 2.7 \\\\\n&= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= θ^{6}e^{-11θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "bd63ff93-762d-5d7d-be8d-9701130b9fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11123" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1$,\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.0, \\, 4.5, \\,5.1$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)((θ + 1)x_{i}^{- θ - 2}) \\\\\n&= (θ + 1)^{n} \\cdot ∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{- θ - 2} \\\\\n&= (θ + 1)^{n}(∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{- θ - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∏_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 2.0 \\cdot 4.5 \\cdot 5.1 = 45.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = (\\theta+1)^{3} (45.9)^{-\\theta-2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58a0ec5-d370-5b08-8b35-9d6e58770be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ^{4}(33.6)^{- θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191093} {:answer/id #uuid "ed701c40-3275-56a0-9d82-0895adf0c693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ^{3}(33.6)^{- θ - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191094} {:answer/id #uuid "f1808a54-54a8-5581-8838-f23c49a9305a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ^{33.6}(4)^{- θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191095} {:answer/id #uuid "00c515da-8777-525e-bf01-7a36de740485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ^{33.6}(4)^{- θ - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191096} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5694b0-2319-5762-8044-e523c01a6149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ^{33.6}(3)^{- θ - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191097}] :answer-field/id #uuid "16f75826-a1b7-51cc-b472-78495d53facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191092}] :question/id #uuid "3058acb9-d471-587a-a178-41dd1d63905c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159849" :question/problem "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; θ) = θx^{- θ - 1}, x \\ge 1$,\nwhere $θ$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1, 3.2, 5.0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
